<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      docs/plan/03 § Providers — the implementation assembly: "grains, reconcilers, the chart
      bindings". A reconciler, an action handler and a chart binding; no grain (see the .Contracts
      .csproj).

      ⚠ ITS ENTIRE REFERENCE SET IS ITS OWN .Contracts. Everything else — IResourceReconciler,
      IResourceActionHandler, ReconcileContext, KubeCommand — arrives through it.
      docs/plan/07 § The enforcement seam: "Providers never call the engine." Here that is mechanical
      rather than reviewed, because CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts does not reference
      CyberCloud.Authorization.Contracts, so ICheckGrain is not nameable from this assembly at all.

      ⚠ THAT MECHANICAL GUARANTEE COSTS THIS PROVIDER SOMETHING NO EARLIER ONE PAID, AND IT IS WORTH
      NAMING WHERE THE REFERENCE SET IS DECLARED. A console is a credential path — docs/plan/19
      § Auditing exists for that reason — and the `connect` action would like to refuse a caller who is
      not the console's own principal. It cannot: ActionContext carries no CallerContext, and this
      assembly could not name one usefully if it did. The permission check that DOES happen is the
      registry's, above this assembly, and the residual gap is
      charts/managed/cloud-shell/conformance.yaml § owed, `connect-cannot-see-its-caller`.

      ⚠ NO KubernetesClient. docs/plan/03 § Assembly graph rules, rule 3: nothing above
      CyberCloud.Kubernetes names a k8s.Models type. Every object this provider renders — a
      PersistentVolumeClaim, a ServiceAccount, a NetworkPolicy and a Pod — HAS a k8s.Models type,
      which is new: eleven families before this one rendered custom resources where the temptation
      does not arise. They are built as JsonObject anyway, and rule 3 is why.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Providers.Terminal.Contracts/CyberCloud.Providers.Terminal.Contracts.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="CyberCloud.Analyzers — see CyberCloud.Core.csproj for why this is not a reference">
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../CyberCloud.Analyzers/CyberCloud.Analyzers.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
